artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a tissue modeled as a spatial domain populated by cells that divide, differentiate, and migrate stochastically. A clone is the set of all descendants of a single progenitor cell, and the clonal dynamics is described by the joint stochastic evolution of clone size, clone composition (across differentiation states), and clone spatial extent over time. Let each cell have a position in the tissue, a differentiation state drawn from a finite hierarchy (e.g. stem, progenitor, differentiated), and local interactions with neighboring cells determined by (i) the geometry of the tissue (its dimensionality, curvature, and boundary structure), (ii) the local neighborhood of each cell (e.g. contact topology, density, mechanical pressure), and (iii) localized signaling niches that bias division and fate-choice rates.

Formulate and analyze a tractable stochastic spatial model of clonal dynamics in which division, differentiation, and migration rates of each cell depend on its local cellular neighborhood, on the geometry of the surrounding tissue, and on proximity to localized niches. Determine quantitatively how these local spatial effects shape:
(a) the distribution of clone sizes as a function of time;
(b) the composition of clones across differentiation states;
(c) the spatial dispersion and shape of clones (e.g. radius of gyration, fractal dimension, persistence of clonal boundaries).

In particular, identify which local rules (neighbor-dependent division/fate biases, geometry-driven anisotropies, niche-mediated retention) are necessary and sufficient to reproduce empirically observed scaling laws for clone size distributions and clone spatial structure, and characterize universal versus model-specific features of the resulting clonal statistics.

